====================================================================================================
  onsetdiag sha256 ad337adc7a8ecb70109b24bdabc4942f8613e45e90592f6921977863b22b4041
  rerunlib sha256  6a7ddf42242d5101086741e3a341db9711b2d47fdbfd60710be9229de8895b07
  python 3.12.3   numpy 2.5.3   platform Linux-7.0.0-38-generic-x86_64-with-glibc2.39   workers 2
  --n39 runs/rerun-analysis-20261025-051314   --twenty runs/rerun-analysis-20261027-185918
  writes nothing; no seed (nothing random); point S-slopes only, no bootstrap

  What this prints, in plain words. The onset of a curve is where a single bend (a
  hinge) fits the S-slope-against-log-r curve best, chosen among r = 0.75, 1.0, 1.25,
  1.5 and 2.0. The pooled onset is the bend of the AVERAGE curve over the three
  objectives, not the average of their bends. For isotropic d = 80 the script prints
  each curve, how badly a bend at each candidate fits it (RSS: smaller fits better),
  where the best bend lands, and a text plot, for ONSET (probe beta) and ONSET-CAL
  (fixed beta). Two facts about the candidates: a bend at 0.75 is just a straight
  line; a bend at 2.0 has only r = 3.0 to its right, so it always fits r = 3.0
  exactly and is judged on the first five points alone. R1 checks that the committed
  onsets follow from the committed slopes; D checks onset_bp's choice by a second
  route; SCOPE lists every dimension so the d = 80 case can be compared with the rest.

